-- title:
--   Integral with integral inverse over K[j] implies order zero
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the project's sense: a valuation subring $v.\mathrm{toValuationSubring}$ of $F$ that contains the image of $K$ under the structure map, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring. Let $j, x \in F$, suppose $j$ lies in $v.\mathrm{toValuationSubring}$, and suppose that both $x$ and $x^{-1}$ are integral over the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ K\ \{j\}$ of $F$ generated by $j$, i.e. each is a root of a monic polynomial with coefficients in $K[j]$. Then $v.\mathrm{ord}\ x = 0$, where $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the logarithm of the value of the adic valuation attached to $v$ (the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued valuation of the height-one prime of $v.\mathrm{toValuationSubring}$), so that $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is the normalised order of vanishing at $v$, with the value $0$ at $x = 0$ by Lean's inversion convention $0^{-1} = 0$ being consistent with the hypotheses.
--
--   The statement records that an element of $F$ which, together with its inverse, is integral over $K[j]$ is a unit at every place where $j$ is integral, hence has order zero there. It is used in the modular-curve part of the development, for instance in locating the support of divisors of modular units at the cusps and in the height and cuspidal-class computations that cite it.
